Episode-level bootstrap ($B = 10{,}000$) yields 95\% CI $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$ for DAF; per-task CIs confirm $\Delta > 0$ for 10 of 11 types. Cluster bootstrap ($K{=}11$) yields $[73.3\%, 100\%]$; with $K < 40$ this should be interpreted cautiously~\citep{cameron-2008}.

\subsection{Multi-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross-model}

A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{llama3-2024} replication on 7 task types ($N{=}70$) shows the death-avoidance pattern reproduces and intensifies: internal death rate $34.3\%$ vs.\ deterministic $0\%$ (cf.\ Qwen: $42.9\%$ vs.\ $16.3\%$), with zero internal-preferred episodes (24 det-preferred, 46 no-preference). With $N{=}70$ and zero internal-preferred labels, this preliminary pilot demonstrates directional consistency of the death mechanism across model families, not full framework replication, which would require a larger sample and independent environment. Both models are in the 7--8B range; 70B-class models and Reflexion-augmented reasoning represent testable predictions (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}).

\subsection{Agent Improvement Baselines}
\label{sec:agent-improvement}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Death\% & $N$ \\
\midrule
Single-trial baseline & 42.9 & 245 \\
Retry ($3{\times}$) & 44.5 & 191 \\
Reflexion ($3{\times}$) & 31.4 & 220 \\
Deterministic baseline & 16.3 & 245 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Internal-strategy death rates under agent improvement methods. Retry does not reduce death; Reflexion partially reduces death but the gap to deterministic remains large (15.1~pp).}
\label{tab:agent-improvement}
\end{table}

We test whether agent-level improvement reduces death dominance. Retry gives the agent three attempts per episode without self-correction; Reflexion~\citep{reflexion-2023} adds verbal self-reflection between trials (Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement}). Retry does not lower death rate (44.5\% vs.\ 42.9\% baseline): additional attempts without error correction create more failure opportunities. Reflexion reduces death to 31.4\% by rescuing 29.3\% of first-trial deaths (27 of 92), but the gap to deterministic (16.3\%) remains 15.1~pp. Trial~1 death rate under Reflexion (41.8\%) matches the single-trial baseline; the reduction comes from cross-trial self-correction, not from the Reflexion prompt itself. Trial~3 adds minimal rescue beyond Trial~2 (net $+4$). Preliminary Qwen2.5-72B results (4 of 11 tasks) show comparable death rates to 7B (e.g., \texttt{chemistry-mix}: 50\% at both scales), consistent with the hypothesis that death risk is an environmental structural property.

\subsection{Cross-Domain Negative Contrast: MATH}
\label{sec:math-contrast}

On MATH Level~4--5~\citep{math-2021} ($N{=}795$ problems, code interpreter as deterministic modality), internal reasoning and code interpretation achieve near-identical accuracy: $73.5\%$ vs.\ $73.0\%$ ($+0.5$~pp, McNemar $p = 0.81$, 95\% CI $[-2.6, +3.6]$~pp). The label distribution is balanced (107 \texttt{int\_preferred} vs.\ 95 \texttt{det\_preferred}, 74.6\% \texttt{no\_preference}), ruling out routing signal ${\geq}3.6$~pp at $\alpha{=}0.05$. Without catastrophic failure, modality routing confers no advantage.

\subsection{Cross-Environment Contrasts}
\label{sec:cross-env}

We evaluate two additional environments lacking catastrophic death penalties.

\paragraph{ALFWorld.} ALFWorld~\citep{alfworld-2021} is a text-based household environment (6 task types, 135 games, max 50 steps). The two modalities differ in \emph{information access}: the deterministic strategy additionally receives admissible actions (an information asymmetry rather than a computation mode switch). The success rate gap is 27.4\% vs.\ 3.0\%, with 35 deterministic-preferred, 2 internal-preferred, and 98 no-preference labels (96 both-fail). The deterministic advantage stems from the information gap rather than death avoidance, a qualitatively different mechanism that the framework diagnoses without modification.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccccc@{}}
\toprule
Environment & $N$ & Det\% & Int\% & NoPref\% & Death? \\
\midrule
ScienceWorld & 245 & 35.1 & 3.7 & 61.2 & Yes ($-100$) \\
MATH & 795 & 12.0 & 13.5 & 74.6 & No \\
ALFWorld & 135 & 25.9 & 1.5 & 72.6 & No\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Cross-environment label distribution. ScienceWorld exhibits strong deterministic preference driven by death avoidance; MATH shows balanced labels (no routing signal); ALFWorld shows deterministic preference driven by information asymmetry. $\dagger$\,ALFWorld's deterministic advantage stems from action-space pruning, not death avoidance.}
\label{tab:cross-env}
\end{table}

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 tasks, 245 episodes). $\dagger$\,Single random seed (42) per episode pair; see Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness} for 8-seed robustness analysis.}
\label{tab:baselines}
